INT. CRUISE SHIP "SERENISSIMA" - CASINO CASHIER CAGE - NIGHT

A brass cage with a tiny window, stacks of chips behind it, a rotating security camera. Beyond, the casino floor chimes and flashes.

HENRY GAUTHIER, 50s, cashier in a burgundy vest, counts chips with a practiced thumb. Behind the glass, a WOMAN IN A FEATHERED MASK, 30s, waits in a silver gown, an ornate clutch pressed to her ribs. She taps the counter twice.

WOMAN IN FEATHERED MASK
Cash me out, please.

HENRY
Of course, madam. How much?

She slides a rack of plaques through the window. He counts. His eyebrows go up.

HENRY (CONT'D)
That is... a great deal, madam.

WOMAN IN FEATHERED MASK
It was a lucky night.

HENRY
Very lucky. Name for the receipt?

She hesitates. Her clutch shifts. A muffled cough comes from the hallway behind her. A man in a waiter's jacket is leaning there, FINNEGAN MBATHA, 30s, one hand inside his tray cloth.

ZINNIA
Zinnia Delacroix-Obi.

HENRY
(writing)
Delacroix. Spelled the usual way?

ZINNIA
The only way.

He slides a form through. She signs. Behind her, Finnegan nudges a service cart, rolling it quietly against the cage door, while Henry stacks bills into an envelope and licks the flap. She slips her mask up an inch, scanning the camera in the ceiling, and he steps from the doorway with the tray, the cloth peeling back from a small black pistol.

FINNEGAN
Mr. Gauthier. Don't.

Henry freezes mid-lick. The envelope droops.

HENRY
I see.

ZINNIA
He's very polite, isn't he?

FINNEGAN
I try.

HENRY
Madam, I have a family.

ZINNIA
So did the last man.

Henry's hands go up. Finnegan steps in, kicks the cart wide. Zinnia reaches through the window and takes the envelope, then the plaques, then a second tray of cash from under the counter. The camera swivels toward them.

FINNEGAN
Camera.

Zinnia tosses her clutch. It strikes the lens and a puff of grey fills the lens. The red light dies.

ZINNIA
Two minutes.

FINNEGAN
Quiet, now.

They slip out. Henry sits, slowly, on his stool. Pulls a handkerchief from his vest. Dabs his brow. Then, from beneath the register, he lifts a very small silver bell and rings it twice.
